Still getting validity=0. The issue must be in the `compute_max_radii` function. Let me examine it more carefully - the algorithm seems to have a bug where it can reduce all radii to 0.

Looking at the original code:
```python
for i in range(n):
    for j in range(i + 1, n):
        dist = np.sqrt(np.sum((centers[i] - centers[j]) ** 2))

        # If current radii would cause overlap, reduce the larger one
        if radii[i] + radii[j] > dist:
            # Reduce the larger radius proportionally
            if radii[i] > radii[j]:
                radii[i] = dist - radii[j]
            else:
                radii[j] = dist - radii[i]
```

The issue is that this greedy approach can fail - when circles A and B constrain each other, and circles B and C constrain each other, the greedy reduction might not work properly. Also, if all circles are too close, radii can become 0.

